WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.280
So, just based on this, for those who have been working with web apps for a while, you

00:04.280 --> 00:09.040
might already start to realize there's a lot of flexibility with this, because now all

00:09.040 --> 00:13.080
of a sudden, if you have a back end that's not quite ready to integrate all the build

00:13.080 --> 00:17.640
tools and everything you need to get it up and running, you can just on a specific page

00:17.640 --> 00:21.600
when you're proposing to start progressively migrating your apps, you can say on this

00:21.600 --> 00:26.040
page we're going to drop the CDN and we're going to add the basically interactivity

00:26.040 --> 00:30.720
to this page and over time will migrate away and then once we have enough pages that consolidate

00:30.720 --> 00:34.360
that view, then we can actually invest the time to do the build tools.

00:34.360 --> 00:37.880
Because I think a lot of times when we think about migration, a lot of times developers

00:37.880 --> 00:42.520
are thinking like code freeze everything, no new features, and we just stop development.

00:42.520 --> 00:46.400
And we know that practically speaking, that just rarely works for business.

00:46.400 --> 00:51.600
And so this is a really great way for us to basically go ahead and migrate an enterprise

00:51.600 --> 00:58.280
app over to something that might use more modern framework technologies.

00:58.280 --> 01:01.240
Now that said, we have a message here.

01:01.240 --> 01:07.560
Now what if we wanted to, let's say, show or hide it based on whether or not the message

01:07.560 --> 01:09.320
is even or not?

01:09.320 --> 01:13.520
Well, something that View is also known for are their directives.

01:13.520 --> 01:16.560
And so for those who have come from Angular, this might look a little bit familiar.

01:16.560 --> 01:21.800
So the way that it works is that we basically would have a normal HTML element.

01:21.800 --> 01:27.080
So let's say let's wrap this in a P element.

01:27.080 --> 01:29.760
And then this one we're going to say even.

01:29.760 --> 01:33.600
And this one we're going to say odd.

01:33.600 --> 01:38.680
And so the idea here being that what we'd like to do is if the message.length is even,

01:38.680 --> 01:43.040
show the even keyword, otherwise show the odd keyword.

01:43.040 --> 01:47.560
And so one way we can do this is we can use something called view directives.

01:47.560 --> 01:52.360
And the way it works is it's basically an attribute you add on an HTML element, and

01:52.360 --> 01:55.160
it's prefixed with v dash.

01:55.160 --> 02:03.880
So in this case, if I go v dash if equals message.length, and I'll use the modular 2

02:03.880 --> 02:06.800
equals 0 to determine that it's even.

02:06.800 --> 02:11.280
And then otherwise, v else, it'll display the other thing.

02:11.280 --> 02:14.400
So if we refresh this, we'll see, ah, OK, there we go.

02:14.400 --> 02:16.240
We'll notice that it is currently odd.

02:16.240 --> 02:22.440
And so now if I delete the exclamation mark, that should bring us over to even.

02:22.440 --> 02:27.480
And so what we see here is, again, one of the things I talked about when it came to

02:27.480 --> 02:30.480
view is that it builds on top of what you already know.

02:30.480 --> 02:32.560
And so when most people are working with HTML and CSS,

02:32.560 --> 02:37.280
there's a lot of value in extending upon people's existing knowledge when it comes

02:37.280 --> 02:38.680
to extending APIs.

02:38.680 --> 02:42.600
And so in this regard, by basically having the convention of the v dash and

02:42.600 --> 02:44.280
then using the if-else statements.

02:44.280 --> 02:47.560
Again, if you know JavaScript, in fact, you don't even need to know JavaScript.

02:47.560 --> 02:52.840
People would actually understand this property, granted, maybe not this part.

02:52.840 --> 02:54.920
But when people are navigating the HTML,

02:54.920 --> 02:56.400
they would actually understand what's going on here.

02:56.400 --> 03:00.600
Rather than if you had kind of big if-else blogs that have a bunch of functions in it,

03:00.600 --> 03:04.320
which again, I think sometimes we find is a little bit tricky with,

03:04.320 --> 03:08.120
I think, sort of React components, those can be harder to follow.

03:08.120 --> 03:10.960
Especially if you don't have as much experience with JavaScript.

03:10.960 --> 03:16.880
But this here keeps a visually a semantic concise way of telling people what's going on.

03:19.080 --> 03:24.200
Now, the other directive that I want to talk about is this one.

03:25.320 --> 03:28.040
But before we do that, here's a slide to talk about this.

03:28.040 --> 03:31.200
And this is from a while ago from Sansa Cone.

03:31.200 --> 03:36.320
And what it says here is that 99.7% of software development in one requirement.

03:36.360 --> 03:39.320
A user should be able to view a list of items.

03:39.320 --> 03:41.360
And I still think that's incredibly relevant today, right?

03:41.360 --> 03:42.440
That's what we're doing half the time.

03:42.440 --> 03:45.440
We're taking a bunch of data, rendering out a list.

03:45.440 --> 03:50.000
And I don't know about you all, but when I was doing this in React originally,

03:50.000 --> 03:52.600
I realized there's a lot of different ways to do it.

03:52.600 --> 03:54.440
And I know that there's more of a best practice now.

03:54.440 --> 03:57.960
But that can be confusing for users, especially if they have a function that

03:57.960 --> 04:00.720
runs the map or maybe in this case, use the for loop.

04:00.720 --> 04:04.200
There's a lot of kind of deciding what to do.

04:04.200 --> 04:07.920
And so let's go ahead and show how rendering a list works in view.

04:07.920 --> 04:11.880
So in this case, I'm just going to do a list of numbers here, just to keep it easy.

04:11.880 --> 04:15.880
And so one, two, three, four, five, and we have an array here.

04:17.200 --> 04:21.960
And then what we're going to do here is we're just going to do an unordered list.

04:21.960 --> 04:23.800
And then what are we trying to repeat, right?

04:23.800 --> 04:25.600
We're trying to repeat the list item.

04:25.600 --> 04:28.560
So what we're going to have is, if you might want to guess,

04:28.560 --> 04:31.640
is that we have the v4 directive, right?

04:31.640 --> 04:33.920
Using the for loop concept from JavaScript.

04:33.920 --> 04:34.840
And what are we looping for?

04:34.840 --> 04:41.000
For every number in list of numbers, we're going to go ahead and

04:41.000 --> 04:42.080
just put the number.

04:44.240 --> 04:44.600
That's it.

04:46.200 --> 04:49.360
And if you refresh, there you go, done.

04:49.360 --> 04:51.560
Your list is rendered, it's fantastic.

04:54.000 --> 04:58.280
And so what's really nice about this, once again, is that we are trying to extend

04:58.280 --> 05:02.080
upon people's base knowledge of JavaScript without getting too much in the weeds.

05:02.080 --> 05:05.480
Again, the syntax is written in a way that's approachable for

05:05.480 --> 05:07.960
people to sort of like in plain English, really.

05:07.960 --> 05:09.640
For number in list of numbers, print number.

05:09.640 --> 05:11.840
Great, I understand what that means, even if I don't know JavaScript.

05:13.320 --> 05:17.240
The other fun fact about the declarative rendering of the curly braces here is

05:17.240 --> 05:19.000
that it's not meant to just print out variables.

05:19.000 --> 05:21.840
It actually does evaluate JavaScript statements in there.

05:21.840 --> 05:26.560
So if in the case of, let's say we wanted to be really excited about the fact that

05:26.560 --> 05:31.200
it's even, we can actually do two uppercase in here and run it.

05:32.240 --> 05:34.440
And then you'll see that it'll actually transform the text,

05:34.440 --> 05:37.240
basically run the JavaScript inside of it immediately.

05:37.240 --> 05:39.000
So theoretically, you could also do things like, well,

05:39.000 --> 05:43.840
let's just multiply like math.random in here and we refresh this, boom.

05:43.840 --> 05:46.840
I mean, please, we have some interesting numbers up here.

05:46.840 --> 05:49.040
But that's something to consider.

05:49.040 --> 05:51.480
Now, the thing about this is when people discover this,

05:51.480 --> 05:53.080
sometimes they get really carried away.

05:53.080 --> 05:56.120
And we're talking like ternary statements and ternary statements.

05:56.120 --> 05:59.920
And that to me is very, I would say, anti-view ethos in that we're trying to

05:59.920 --> 06:02.240
make it easy for people to understand the code base, right?

06:02.240 --> 06:04.960
So I would say when it comes to basically inlining your JavaScript

06:04.960 --> 06:08.240
inside of these double curly braces, I would say keep it to a minimum and

06:08.240 --> 06:10.640
try to abstract that logic as much as humanly possible.

06:10.640 --> 06:14.080
So the question we have here is in regard to the differences between the View2Way

06:14.080 --> 06:15.120
and the View3Way.

06:15.120 --> 06:18.920
So the View3Way, as we see here, is that you can abstract a helper function from

06:18.920 --> 06:25.600
View or if for those familiar with the View2Way, we had View, it was NewView.

06:25.600 --> 06:28.760
You would instantiate a NewView app this way.

06:29.760 --> 06:34.040
And so part of the reason this approach has changed is because View3 has

06:34.040 --> 06:36.680
become a lot about becoming modular and performant.

06:36.680 --> 06:40.520
In other words, being able to split apart things that you don't need.

06:40.520 --> 06:44.320
With this sort of NewView approach, basically you have everything instantiated

06:44.320 --> 06:47.200
with it and it was a lot harder from a bundling perspective,

06:47.200 --> 06:49.280
like thinking of it from a library maintenance perspective.

06:49.280 --> 06:52.760
That was a lot of the motivation behind this new approach,

06:52.760 --> 06:56.640
which you'll see a lot more in View3CodeBases is that there's a lot of helper

06:56.640 --> 06:59.720
functions which we'll actually see in this workshop that allow you to basically

06:59.720 --> 07:02.600
break apart pieces of View that are helpful in certain instances.

07:02.600 --> 07:06.760
And otherwise, it'll know to either drop certain things and optimize itself away.

